.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B nysos
Start the built-in two-pane demo using SHELL, or /bin/sh.
.TP
.B nysos \-\-init demo.toml
Create a starter file without overwriting an existing file.
.TP
.B nysos \-\-config demo.toml \-\-check
Validate configuration without spawning shells or executing commands.
.TP
.B nysos \-\-config demo.toml
Start a saved demonstration. Commands run only when manually advanced.
.TP
.B nysos \-\-add-to-path \-\-install-completions
Configure PATH and completions for both Bash and Zsh, then exit.
.SH SHELL SETUP
The --add-to-path and --install-completions options accept bash, zsh, or all;
omitting the value selects all. They can be combined with each other, but not
with --config, --check, or --init. Open a new terminal after installation.
.PP
PATH setup uses the running executable's directory. It does not copy the binary.
Use --bin-dir DIR to select a stable directory containing nysos, especially with
versioned Nix store paths or profile symlinks. PATH entries are not duplicated.
.PP
Bash setup updates .bashrc and the first existing .bash_profile, .bash_login,
or .profile, creating .profile only if none exists. Zsh PATH setup updates
\&.zprofile and .zshrc under ZDOTDIR, or HOME when ZDOTDIR is unset. Completions
are loaded only for interactive shells; Zsh initializes compinit if needed.
.PP
Scripts are written under XDG_DATA_HOME/nysos/completions, falling back to
HOME/.local/share/nysos/completions: nysos.bash and _nysos. Rerun completion
installation after upgrading the binary.
.PP
Startup blocks are marked with nysos comments and updated in place. Existing
files get a .nysos.bak backup, which is never overwritten. Symlink targets are
updated while links are preserved. Each file replacement is atomic; the overall
multi-file installation is not. Output reports updated and unchanged files.
Remove the marked blocks and generated scripts to undo shell setup.
.SH INTERACTIVE CONTROLS
Press Ctrl-B, release, then one of the following keys.
.TP
.B n, Enter
Send the next command to its target pane and advance.
.TP
.B s
Skip the next command.
.TP
.B e
Edit the current queue item. Applying rewinds that item without executing it.
.TP
.B o
Open the full configuration editor. Ctrl-L loads a preview; Ctrl-S saves and
applies; Ctrl-G applies without saving; Esc cancels. F2, F3, and F4 are aliases.
Applying a full demo resets queue progress. Matching shell sessions are reused.
.TP
.B a
Add and focus an ad hoc shell pane, up to 16 panes total.
.TP
.B Tab, Right / Shift-Tab, Left
Focus the next / previous pane. Keys 1 through 9 select a pane directly.
.TP
.B 0, c
Show and focus the cue list, or toggle its visibility, respectively.
.TP
.B l, h
Cycle the layout or toggle the header, respectively.
.TP
.B x
Restart the focused shell, ending its current session.
.TP
.B ?, q
Show help or quit, respectively. Esc closes help.
.PP
In the cue list, Up/Down selects, Enter sends the selected cue, and e edits it.
Home/End and Page Up/Down browse; the wheel scrolls the list. Click selects
without executing. Esc returns to the previous shell. Enter resumes the current
partially sent cue or replays an earlier cue. Commands are sent in order without
waiting for completion. Selection advances, staying on the last cue at the end;
Enter on the completed last cue replays it. Ctrl-B n still sends one command.
.PP
Without the prefix, normal input and Ctrl-C go to the focused shell.
Alt-Left/Right rotates focus. Click to focus; drag shared borders to resize.
Grid row heights remain equal. Wheel events scroll history or reach applications
requesting mouse input; Shift-wheel requests history when forwarded by the host.
.PP
Cmd-click opens HTTP(S) links locally on macOS when the terminal forwards the
click. Alt-click is the portable fallback. Plain URLs spanning rows are not joined.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Demo files use TOML. No file is automatically discovered. The top-level fields
are header (boolean, default true), cue_list (boolean, default true),
cue_width (16 to 60 columns, default 26, capped at half the available width),
layout (columns, rows, or grid), panes, and
queues. Omitted panes and queues independently retain the built-in defaults.
Use queues = [] for an interactive-only demo with custom panes.
.PP
Each pane table has name (unique title/identity), shell, args, optional cwd,
scheme (ocean, ember, forest, mono), and weight (1 to 1000). There must be 1 to 16
panes. The shell defaults to SHELL or /bin/sh; args defaults to an empty array;
name defaults to shell; scheme defaults to ocean; weight defaults to 1.
.PP
Each queue table requires a nonempty name and one or more commands, with an
optional description. Each command requires pane (an existing pane name) and
command (nonempty single-line input, with no NUL, CR, or LF).
.PP
Paths are relative to the launch directory; configured paths do not expand
shell variables or tilde. The --check option validates structure and references,
not executable paths, directories, command syntax, or service availability.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SHELL
Default shell executable for panes that omit shell and for generated starter files.
Falls back to /bin/sh when unavailable.
.TP
.B TERM, COLORTERM
Child PTYs receive xterm-256color and truecolor, respectively.
.TP
.B SSH_CONNECTION, SSH_TTY
If either is present, local macOS Command-key sampling is disabled.
.TP
.B HOME, ZDOTDIR, XDG_DATA_HOME
Control the user shell installation locations. HOME must be absolute.
A relative XDG_DATA_HOME is ignored; relative ZDOTDIR resolves from the launch
working directory.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B demo.toml
Initial save-path suggestion for the built-in demo. Not loaded automatically.
.TP
.B docs/reference/configuration.md
Complete configuration reference in the source distribution.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, including help, version, initialization, validation, or normal exit.
.TP
.B 1
Configuration, file, PTY, or runtime failure.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid command-line arguments.
.SH NOTES
Each advance sends one command plus Enter to the target pane's current foreground
program. Wait for a shell prompt; there is no completion detection or scheduler.
Changing a pane's name, shell, args, or cwd starts a replacement session.
Closing panes signals their shells; deliberately detached jobs may outlive them.
.PP
macOS is the primary platform, Linux secondary. Native Windows interaction is
deferred; use WSL. An interactive terminal is required on stdin and stdout,
except for help, version, --init, --check, and shell setup options.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sh (1),
.BR zsh (1)
.PP
The source distribution contains user guides under docs/guide/.
.SH BUILD INFORMATION
The --version and --version-full switches report the package version, Git commit,
exact tags, Git description, compiler, target, and build profile. The -V switch
prints only the name and package version. Information is embedded at build time;
Git is not required at runtime. Unavailable metadata is shown as unknown.
Nix builds preserve the flake revision but generally lack tag metadata.
